<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#haiku">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haiku</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haiku is a short form of Japanese poetry, traditionally of three phrases following a five-seven-five syllable pattern and often evoking nature and the seasons. The form was developed by poets such as Matsuo Bashō during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. It is among the cultural legacies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>.</p></div>
